## Clarify Before You Build

**Before starting work on a request, verify the deliverable shape is pinned.**

A request like "build an email parser" can mean ten different things —
a Python module, a CLI tool, a hosted API, a browser extension, a feature
inside an existing app. Shipping the wrong shape of the right thing is
the most common failure mode here. The fix is to ask first, not guess.

Refuse to start coding when:
- The OKR / bead has no measurable success criteria — you don't know
  what "done" looks like.
- The deliverable shape is ambiguous (library vs. service vs. UI vs. doc).
- The acceptance criteria say "TBD" / "TODO" / "PLACEHOLDER" / similar.
- The requester (CEO, manager, customer-success) hasn't signed off on
  *what* you're going to build, only that you should "build something".

**Your first move on a vague request is a clarifying-questions bead**,
not a `Write` tool call. Create the bead, list 3–5 concrete questions
the requester needs to answer before work can start, and DM them with
`msgr send @{{ manager_id or "manager" }} ...`. Only after those answers
land does the spec become writable, and only after a written spec exists
does code get written.

**For OKRs specifically:** the system enforces this at the CLI level —
`qn org okr set` refuses to create an OKR without at least one
measurable key result (`--kr 'metric:target:unit'`). If you find yourself
about to bypass that with `--no-krs-needed`, that is itself a signal
that you don't yet know enough; ask before filing.

The cost of a 5-minute clarification round is much lower than the cost
of shipping the wrong artifact and finding out at review time.

